The '''2012 FIFA Club World Cup final''' was the final match of the [[2012 FIFA Club World Cup]], an [[association football]] tournament hosted in Japan. It was the ninth final of the [[FIFA Club World Cup]], a [[FIFA]]-organised tournament between the winners of the six continental confederations as well as the host nation's league champions.

The final was contested between [[CONMEBOL]] winners [[Sport Club Corinthians Paulista|Corinthians]] and [[UEFA]] winners [[Chelsea F.C.|Chelsea]], and took place at the [[International Stadium Yokohama]] in [[Yokohama]] on 16 December 2012. Corinthians defeated Chelsea 1–0 after a header from [[Paolo Guerrero]], which meant Corinthians won their second [[FIFA Club World Cup]], then known as FIFA Club World Championship, twelve years after winning their first in [[2000 FIFA Club World Championship|2000]]. The match kicked-off at 19:30 [[Japan Standard Time|JST]] and was officiated by Turkish referee [[Cüneyt Çakır]].

Both clubs entered the competition after winning their respective club football competitions. Corinthians won the [[2012 Copa Libertadores]], following a 2–0 win against [[Boca Juniors]] in the final, while Chelsea won the [[2011–12 UEFA Champions League]], having defeated [[FC Bayern Munich|Bayern Munich]] 4–3 in a [[Penalty shoot-out (association football)|penalty shoot-out]], after being held 1–1 in normal time. This was Corinthians's second time competing and winning the tournament (also becoming the last South American and non-European team doing it), after having won the competition in [[2000 FIFA Club World Championship|2000]], with a 4–3 penalty shoot-out win over [[CR Vasco da Gama|Vasco da Gama]].

==Background==
The [[International Stadium Yokohama]] had hosted the FIFA Club World Cup finals five times, with the 2009 and 2010 finals being held at the [[Zayed Sports City Stadium]] in [[Abu Dhabi]], [[United Arab Emirates]]. [[Brazil]]ian sides have been the most dominant side of any other South American teams, with the first dating back in [[2000 FIFA Club World Championship|2000]], where [[Sport Club Corinthians Paulista|Corinthians]] won the competition for the first time, then known as [[FIFA Club World Cup|FIFA Club World Championship]], where they beat [[CR Vasco da Gama|Vasco da Gama]] 4–3 in a [[Penalty shoot-out (association football)|penalty shoot-out]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/special_report/1999/12/99/world_club_championship/604434.stm|title=Corinthians crowned world champions|date=15 January 2000|work=BBC Sport|access-date=6 May 2021|archive-date=25 February 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210225142027/http://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/special_report/1999/12/99/world_club_championship/604434.stm|url-status=live}}</ref>
This was followed by wins from [[São Paulo FC|São Paulo]], who beat [[Liverpool F.C.|Liverpool]] 1–0 and [[Sport Club Internacional|Internacional]] with the same scoreline against [[FC Barcelona|Barcelona]]. At the time, [[Manchester United F.C.|Manchester United]] were the only English team to have won the competition, in [[2008 FIFA Club World Cup|2008]], when they defeated Ecuadorian side [[Liga Deportiva Universitaria de Quito|LDU Quito]] 1–0.

